---
import LabShell from "../../components/lab/LabShell.astro";
---
<LabShell title="Voronoi letter-logo" current="voronoi-letters" section="lab"
  intro="Type a letter, get a logo built from Voronoi cells inside its outline. The outline comes
  from a real font (Geist Bold) but only as an invisible clip boundary — no typographic detail
  shows, the inside is entirely Voronoi. Each cell is inset toward its own center, which is what
  creates the gap between shapes — no line is drawn, it's background color showing through. Cells
  default to one shared color from the Color Library; click any shape to paint it on its own.
  Crop freeform or to a circle, export SVG/PNG.">

  <div class="lab-section">
    <span class="lab-status lab-status--live">live</span>
    <p class="lab-source">Built directly in this repo — public/lab-tools/voronoi-letters.html +
      public/lab-tools/voronoi-letters/ (no org-os monorepo counterpart, unlike the hero editor) ·
      <a class="link" href="/lab-tools/voronoi-letters.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">open full-page ↗</a></p>
    <div class="lab-frame" style="height: 90vh;">
      <iframe src="/lab-tools/voronoi-letters.html" title="Voronoi letter-logo generator" style="width: 100%; height: 100%; border: 0;"></iframe>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="lab-note">
    <strong>Note:</strong> any letter Geist Bold has a glyph for works — the font supplies the
    outline, nothing else, so this isn't a per-letter design task the way the earlier
    stroke-skeleton approach was. Cell placement is auto-seeded inside the glyph (rejection
    sampling + point-in-path, correctly leaving holes like the inside of an "O" empty), then
    draggable per-site if the auto layout doesn't land well for a given letter.
  </div>
</LabShell>
